>
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This site publishes the working notes of the
    <a href="https://github.com/zmaril/zdl-experiments">zdl-experiments</a>
    repository — a collection of research threads on the mathematics of
    partner dance.
  </p>
  <p>
    The program builds on prior work at
    <a href="https://www.zacksdancelab.com">Zack's Dance Lab</a>, in particular
    Maril's
    <a href="https://www.zacksdancelab.com/blog/a-new-kind-of-dance-science"
      >“A New Kind of Dance Science”</a
    >, which counts two-dancer hand connections via set partitions: B(4) = 15
    partition classes, extended by per-arm hammerlock states to 240 candidates
    and filtered to 157 physically feasible positions. That result treats a
    connection as pure combinatorics; the experiments here probe what it leaves
    out — the topology of crossed and wrapped arms (knot theory), the anatomy
    of the grips themselves (contact regions, taxonomies), what a grip permits
    mechanically (kinematics), and whether the models survive contact with
    photographs of real dancers (pose estimation).
  </p>
  <p>
    The notes are honest working documents, not finished publications:
    conjectures, half-built code, and negative results stay in. Each research
    thread keeps a <code>NOTES.md</code> in its directory of the repository,
    and this site is rebuilt from whatever notes exist — see the
    <a href="https://github.com/zmaril/zdl-experiments/tree/master/site"
      >site README</a
    > for the publishing convention.
  </p>
  <p>
    Errors in the math, uncataloged grips, and other corrections are welcome as
    issues on
    <a href="https://github.com/zmaril/zdl-experiments/issues">GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
